This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
## 1. Primary Request and Intent

This session covered building and iteratively refining a first-run onboarding wizard for the Workroom macOS app (GitHub issue #151: "New install setup — Need a great looking startup intro and flow for first time usage"), followed by a series of related UI polish requests. In chronological order, the user's explicit requests were:

1. Plan and review the onboarding wizard feature (via plan mode + `/plan-eng-review` skill)
2. Implement the approved plan
3. "leave this" (re: Window-menu suppression follow-up) / "tests should never use the real config" — two directives after I reported deviations from the plan
4. "run it" — launch the app to demo the feature
5. "only the onboarding wizard should open on a fresh start. Only open main window when the wizard is closed, or finished."
6. "run all the onboarding tests again" (repeated twice at different points)
7. "the nav dots on welcome screen shift when left and right buttons are added/removed. keep the dots centered so they don't shift"
8. "replace the 'sidebar' feature item with a version control item with a title of 'Version Control Built in'"
9. "add a 'Live activity + notifications' item."
10. "how does this all work for a branch new user? Can I try it?"
11. "rerun the UI tests" (multiple times)
12. "remove the title bar from welcome window"
13. Mid-turn: "in 'nothing selected' message, show workroom logo in place of generic terminal icon"
14. Mid-turn: "make it twice as big, and monochrome with no border" (re: the logo)
15. "the logo on that screen is bad quality" — required root-causing blurriness from upscaling a low-res source
16. "I've changed the 'nothing selected' text. Show relevant keyboard shortcuts in brackets next to open and create workroom text"
17. "close right sibar when no workroom is open" [sic] — most recent request, currently being verified

Throughout, the implicit standing intent was: every change must be verified via `make app-generate` → `make app-format` → `make app-build` → `make app-lint` (must stay clean) → relevant unit/UI tests → visual confirmation via screenshot/manual launch before considering it done. The user also cares about correctness at the root-cause level (rejected workarounds twice: once for the config-leak test issue, once implicitly by asking to rerun tests after the launch-behavior change, which surfaced a real regression).

## 2. Key Technical Concepts

- SwiftUI `Scene` composition: `WindowGroup(for:)`, `Window(_:id:)`, `.defaultLaunchBehavior(.presented/.suppressed/.automatic)`, `.windowResizability(.contentSize)`, `.windowStyle(.hiddenTitleBar)`
- `SceneBuilder` does NOT support `if/else` control flow between `Scene` values (confirmed by trying it — compile error), unlike `ViewBuilder`
- `Defaults` package (`sindresorhus/Defaults`) for persisted `UserDefaults`-backed keys — shared across all windows in the app, a key architectural constraint
